        <entry>
          <string>textFontSize</string>
          <int>50</int>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <string>RectHeight</string>
          <int>18</int>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <string>Label</string>
          <string>About the CPU</string>
        </entry>
        <entry>
          <string>RectWidth</string>
          <int>62</int>
        </entry>
      </elementAttributes>
      <pos x="-12860" y="-1700"/>
    </visualElement>
    <visualElement>
      <elementName>Text</elementName>
      <elementAttributes>
        <entry>
          <string>Description</string>
          <string>This is a single-cycle RV32I processor. In addition to the base instruction set, it also implements
the Ziscr extension and contains a debug module.

Although it is single-cycle, it is organized according to pipeline stages. 
Tunnels that cross stages are aliased as prefixed with their destination stage; for example, alu-out
becomes IF-alu-out when crossing into the IF stage. Because this CPU is single-cycle, these tunnels
are equivilent, and at this point it is simply an organizational tool.

Additionally, this is a Von Neumann architecture, and so instruction memory and data memory are
merged. This makes it look slightly different than some educational datapath diagrams you might
see, which sometimes show the Harvard style.</string>
        </entry>
      </elementAttributes>
      <pos x="-12840" y="-1680"/>
    </visualElement>
    <visualElement>
      <elementName>Text</elementName>
      <elementAttributes>
        <entry>
          <string>Description</string>
          <string>TODO: scrap pipeline stage division
R_rs1 - done
